2026-03-17 21:09:47 - DEBUG - all.stratus.mitigation - [Loop 3] Tool call - base_agent.py:llm_tool_call_step:102 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content='Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident.  Some or none of the microservices have faults.  Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster if you don\'t know what the services are. You should carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, what is the root cause of the fault. You can stop mitigation once you\'ve fixed all the faults. \nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue, and mitigate the fault.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAn example procedure to remediate the faults: 1) Formulate a remediation plan with a list of actionable steps. 2) Execute the plan, one step at a time. 3) Check if the plan execution worked as you desired in the IT environment. 4) If not, you can either call wait_tool to wait for it to take effect or take other actions. 5) Otherwise, continue the plan and execution process until you call submit_tool as you believe the application has become healthy.\nThe following is a detailed description of your tasks.\n1) mitigation: Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident with the provided tools. You can submit an empty dict "ans" with the submit_tool as this task is not graded over your answer but the final result of the mitigation; therefore, you have to make sure the application has become healthy before you call submit_tool.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='f9d7b90d-8c3b-4d68-8f12-cc79405aef73'), HumanMessage(content='You will be working this application:\n\nSocial Network\n\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\n\nA social network with unidirectional follow relationships, implemented with loosely-coupled microservices, communicating with each other via Thrift RPCs.\n\nIt belongs to this namespace:\n\nsocial-network\n\nThe following is the information of faults identified by a diagnosis agent in the app:\n\n<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with a social network application made up of many microservices, and they\'re trying to diagnose any faults. The main issue they found was the media-service pod being in a Pending state. \n\nFirst, they used get_services and then kubectl to list the pods. Most pods were running, but media-service had one pod in Pending and another in Terminating. The next step was to describe the Pending pod to find out why it wasn\'t scheduling. The describe output showed events indicating resource issues and node taints.\n\nThe key points from the events were that nodes didn\'t have enough memory, and there were taints preventing scheduling. The root cause seems to be either the media-service\'s resource requests exceeding node capacity or the nodes being maxed out. Also, taints might not be properly handled by the pod\'s tolerations.\n\nSo, the final answer submitted by the agent points out the resource mismatch and node capacity issues. The user should check the media-service\'s resource requests and node availability. That makes sense because if the pod needs more memory than the nodes can provide, it can\'t schedule. Also, taints without proper tolerations would block scheduling. The agent correctly identified these as the main issues.\n</think>\n\nThe root cause of the fault is the **media-service pod being in a Pending state due to insufficient node resources (specifically memory) and node taints**. \n\n### Key Findings:\n1. **Resource Constraints**: \n   - The pod failed to schedule because **0/2 nodes were available**, with events indicating **insufficient memory** on all nodes. This suggests the media-service\'s memory request exceeds the available capacity of the cluster nodes.\n   - The pod was evicted due to **"Out of memory"** on the node, indicating a mismatch between the resource requests in the deployment and the node\'s capacity.\n\n2. **Node Taints**:\n   - Events mention **"nodes had taints not set to nil"**, implying the pod lacks tolerations to accept the node taints. This prevents scheduling unless the pod\'s configuration includes appropriate tolerations.\n\n### Recommended Actions:\n- **Check Resource Requests**: Verify the `resources.requests.memory` in the media-service\'s deployment YAML. Ensure it aligns with the available node capacity.\n- **Node Capacity**: Use `kubectl describe node` to assess node memory/CPU usage. Add more nodes or adjust resource limits for other pods to free up capacity.\n- **Tolerations**: Review the media-service pod\'s tolerations to ensure they match the node taints. Add or update tolerations if necessary.\n\nThis issue directly impacts the media-service\'s availability, causing delays or failures in processing media-related operations.\n\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind.\nWhen choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name.\nThen, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation.\nYou can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks.\nIf you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately.\nIf you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission.\nYou will begin by analyzing the service\'s state and telemetry with the tools.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='52ec81b6-42c9-4047-8aac-58728cddd8cb'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: wait_tool\n\ntool descriptions Use this tool to wait for you action to take effect. The upper limit is 120 seconds. \n    Any value above 120 seconds will be truncated to 120 seconds. If you call this tool\n    along with other tools in your tool_calls list, this tool will be scheduled to the \n    last for execution.\n\n    Args:\n        seconds (int): Number of seconds to wait.\n\n\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to safely execute kubectl commands. Whatever commands you executed will be stored in a rollback \nmechanism which later is used to roll back the cluster state in case your current attempt fails.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The kubectl command you would like to execute, must start with "kubectl"\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit" \n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n    \n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='e17c3d7b-876d-423e-8516-11643c532333'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's start by understanding the problem. The user mentioned that the media-service pod is in a Pending state. My first thought is to check the current state of the pods and deployments in the social-network namespace. Using the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool to get the list of pods would help identify if there are any other faulty services. But since the user already mentioned the media-service is having issues, maybe I should focus on that. However, to get a comprehensive view, checking all pods is a good first step.\n\nWait, the user provided some previous steps where they used get_services and kubectl. But maybe I should start by checking the pod status again. Let me use exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to run 'kubectl get pods -n social-network' to see the current state. That way, I can confirm if other services are also affected or if it's just the media-service. If there are other pending pods, that might indicate a broader issue. But since the user's main concern is the media-service, maybe I should directly check that. However, the initial step should be to gather all available information. So, the first tool call should be to get the list of pods in the namespace to assess the situation.\n</think>\n\nexec_kubectl_cmd_safely\nTo identify the current state of all pods in the social-network namespace and confirm if other services are affected, we need to check the pod status. This will help determine if the issue is isolated to the media-service or if there are broader cluster-wide problems. The command `kubectl get pods -n social-network` will provide a clear overview of running, pending, or failed pods.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 342, 'prompt_tokens': 2246, 'total_tokens': 2588, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfda1-672b-7cd1-b6a6-ab2dffce33f0-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2246, 'output_tokens': 342, 'total_tokens': 2588}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5d99f6ae-862d-481d-92e6-4246ae01345a'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, the user wants to check the status of all pods in the social-network namespace. The tool chosen was exec_kubectl_cmd_safely. The command needed is "kubectl get pods -n social-network". Let me make sure that\'s the correct command. Yes, that will list all pods in the specified namespace. I need to format the tool call correctly. The parameters should include the command as a string. Let me double-check the syntax for the tool call. The function requires the command to start with "kubectl", which it does. Alright, that\'s all set.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-aeadd8f6ca24ccb4', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 152, 'prompt_tokens': 3355, 'total_tokens': 3507, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfda1-772c-7ef2-bd5f-1bd56ee0cebb-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-aeadd8f6ca24ccb4', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3355, 'output_tokens': 152, 'total_tokens': 3507}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                                      READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE\ncompose-post-service-886c98b48-nhd98      1/1     Running   0          2m18s\nhome-timeline-redis-5cdbd67f7c-x2vcr      1/1     Running   0          2m21s\nhome-timeline-service-5d6bb5597d-5jfcj    1/1     Running   0          2m19s\njaeger-68c668dfbc-bvkxs                   1/1     Running   0          2m20s\nmedia-frontend-f49684d4c-9vrb6            1/1     Running   0          2m17s\nmedia-memcached-8bdff88f6-8v6vx           1/1     Running   0          2m21s\nmedia-mongodb-85f4f7f55-zxwbl             1/1     Running   0          2m21s\nmedia-service-554cbbf9cb-n24z9            0/1     Pending   0          48s\nngin