check the node ports. For example, run `kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath=\'{.items[*].status.addresses[?(@.type=="InternalIP")].address}\'` to get node IPs, then check each node\'s port usage. But that\'s complicated. Alternatively, maybe the user can just update the hostPort to 9101 and see if that works. \n\nSince the error is about no free ports for 9100, changing it to 9101 might resolve the issue. So the next step is to update the deployment\'s hostPort to 9101. Let me use exec_kubectl_cmd_safely to edit the deployment. But wait, the user can\'t use interactive commands like kubectl edit. So I need to generate a YAML patch to update the hostPort. \n\nThe command would be to apply a patch to the deployment. So the correct tool is exec_kubectl_cmd_safely with the command to apply the patch. Let me construct that command.\n</think>\n\nexec_kubectl_cmd_safely\nTo resolve the "no free ports" error, we need to update the **hostPort** in the media-service deployment from `9100` to an unused port (e.g., `9101`). This will allow the pod to schedule on a node with an available port. The command will modify the deployment\'s pod spec to use the new port. \n\nThe command to update the deployment is:  \n`kubectl patch deployment media-service -n social-network -p \'{"spec":{"template":{"spec":{"containers":[{"name":"media-service","ports":[{"containerPort":9090,"hostPort":9101,"protocol":"TCP"}]}]}}}}\'`', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 581, 'prompt_tokens': 5699, 'total_tokens': 6280, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfda1-f445-70a2-8772-38334ce778fa-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5699, 'output_tokens': 581, 'total_tokens': 6280}), HumanMessage(content='Now genera